{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 11, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III was a prominent nobleman who ruled the County of Tripoli during the twelfth century. He served as regent for the Kingdom of Jerusalem for many years while the king suffered from leprosy. This role placed him at the center of political power within the Crusader states. His life was filled with conflict and diplomatic maneuvering as he navigated the complex relationships between Latin Christians and Muslim rulers.\n\n*Early Life and Inheritance*\nRaymond was born into a powerful family that held significant lands in the Levant. His father died when he was young so he inherited the county under the guardianship of his mother. He later claimed the regency of Antioch through his mother's lineage but eventually returned to Tripoli to focus his efforts there. He grew up during a time of intense warfare between the Crusader states and the forces of Nur ad-Din. His early years were marked by attempts to secure his borders against Muslim expansion while maintaining alliances with other Latin lords.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nThe king Baldwin IV contracted leprosy at a young age which left him unable to rule effectively. Raymond III stepped forward to serve as regent and protector of the realm. He managed state affairs while the king recovered from various illnesses. Many nobles opposed his influence and sought to diminish his power through court intrigue. Raymond faced accusations of treason from rival factions who wanted to control the throne themselves. He tried to arrange a marriage between the king and a suitable princess to secure an heir but these plans often failed due to political opposition. His long tenure as regent made him the most powerful man in the kingdom for a time.\n\n*Military Campaigns and Death*\nRaymond participated in several major battles against the forces led by Saladin. He commanded troops at the Battle of Montgisard where the Crusaders achieved a great victory against overwhelming odds. He later faced criticism for his decision to make a truce with Saladin which angered many other barons. This diplomatic move was intended to buy time for the kingdom but it ultimately weakened his standing among the hardline faction. He died in 1187 shortly before the disastrous Battle of Hattin. His death removed a key stabilizing figure from the kingdom just before its greatest military defeat. The loss of Tripoli and other strongholds followed soon after his passing. Raymond left behind a legacy of complex leadership during a turbulent era in the history of the Crusades. His actions shaped the political landscape of the Levant for decades to come.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0022097188}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -2.264979e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.0027794088}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.066596635}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.0074293534}, {"token": " prominent", "logprob": -0.82653797}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -0.03191102}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.088237405}, {"token": " who", "logprob": -0.15570551}, {"token": " ruled", "logprob": -0.5370965}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.08329122}, {"token": " County", "logprob": -0.00043133262}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.0027719375}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.02773135}, {"token": " tw", "logprob": -0.016627334}, {"token": "elfth", "logprob": -3.9339143e-06}, {"token": " century", "logprob": -6.4373226e-06}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.0718065}, {"token": " He", "logprob": -0.21498203}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -1.1411059}, {"token": " as", "logprob": -8.547671e-05}, {"token": " reg", "logprob": -0.28429195}, {"token": "ent", "logprob": -1.192093e-07}, {"token": " for", "logprob": -4.017434e-05}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.20170301}, {"token": " Kingdom", "lo